          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01PCQJvQKDEJdRagYEBgGQN7",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/flag-debt.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Flag Debt\\ndescription: Accumulated technical debt from feature flags that persist after their rollout completes, silently multiplying code paths under test.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Flag-Debt\\n  - concept/Technical-Debt\\n  - concept/Testing\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single operational source describing the problem and its testing implications; no corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nFlag debt is the accumulation of feature flags left in the codebase after a feature has fully rolled out or been abandoned. Each flag introduces a conditional code path; when flags are never removed, the number of paths under test grows combinatorially without bound.\\n\\n## The Problem\\n\\nFeature flags enable safe rollout: code deploys behind a dark flag, then gets enabled per-user, per-percentage, or per-environment without redeploying. Once the rollout completes or the feature is abandoned, the flag should be removed. If it is not, it becomes technical debt.\\n\\nThe operational risk is not visibility or control during rollout \\u2014 flags excel at that. The risk is cleanup discipline: code paths multiply silently, and the test surface area grows with every flag that outlives its purpose. A codebase with dozens of old flags becomes expensive to test exhaustively.\\n\\n## Cleanup Discipline\\n\\nManaging flag debt requires:\\n\\n- **Explicit expiration**: flags should have a removal date or completion criterion set at creation, not left indefinitely.\\n- **Tracking**: maintain an inventory of active flags and their rollout status, so drift is visible.\\n- **Automated removal**: once a flag\\u0027s condition is met (100% users enabled, or deprecation date reached), remove it in the next release cycle.\\n- **Testing during cleanup**: ensure that removing the flag does not accidentally disable the feature or break a code path.\\n\\n## Contrast\\n\\nFlag debt is distinct from deliberate, long-lived feature flags (e.g., a kill-switch for a payment processor, or an A/B test that runs indefinitely for business reasons). Those are maintenance costs accepted for operational value. Flag debt is the subset that lingers *without* a justification.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Operational observations on feature flag lifecycle management and testing complexity.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
